SLIDE 6 – TASK 8: ADVANCED NMAP

Advanced Nmap Security Assessment

Objectives:

• Service and version detection
• OS detection
• TCP SYN scanning
• UDP scanning
• NSE vulnerability investigation
• Security analysis of important ports

Commands:

nmap -sV <target-ip>
sudo nmap -O <target-ip>
sudo nmap -sS <target-ip>
sudo nmap -sU --top-ports 20 <target-ip>
nmap --script vuln <target-ip>


SLIDE 7 – TASK 8: SCAN ANALYSIS

Advanced Nmap Analysis

For each important open port:

• Identify the running service
• Understand why the service is required
• Check whether exposure is necessary
• Identify possible security risks
• Suggest security controls

Recommendations:

• Software updates
• Strong authentication
• Firewall configuration
• Access restrictions
• Service hardening
• Monitoring


SLIDE 8 – TASK 9: WIRESHARK

Wireshark Network Traffic Capture

Activities:

• Started packet capture
• Generated normal network traffic
• Captured approximately 5–10 minutes of traffic
• Analyzed network protocols
• Recorded source and destination information

Protocols analyzed:

• DNS
• TCP
• UDP
• ICMP
• ARP
• HTTP


SLIDE 9 – TASK 9: WIRESHARK FILTERS

Wireshark Display Filters

DNS:
dns

TCP:
tcp

UDP:
udp

ICMP:
icmp

ARP:
arp

HTTP:
http

Special Filters:

tcp.flags.syn == 1
tcp.flags.reset == 1
dns.flags.response == 0

Evidence:

[Insert Wireshark Screenshot]


SLIDE 10 – TASK 10: NMAP + WIRESHARK

Nmap + Wireshark Investigation

Objective:

Analyze Nmap scan traffic using Wireshark.

Nmap command:

nmap -sS <target-ip>

Important packets:

• SYN
• SYN/ACK
• RST

Analysis:

SYN → SYN/ACK
Generally indicates an accessible/open TCP service.

SYN → RST
Generally indicates that the port is closed.

No expected response
May indicate filtering, firewall behavior, or packet loss.


